{ id:'enthd-nose-367', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'All of the following **nasal conditions cause granulomata** **except**?',
  options:['Scleroma','TB','Syphilis','Hypertrophic rhinitis'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Hypertrophic rhinitis — the lecture’s own classification tree puts it on the opposite branch from the other three.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` splits chronic infective bacterial rhinosinusitis into two columns and the question is answered by reading them:\n\n| Branch | The deck’s members |\n|---|---|\n| **Chronic NON-specific** | *"Chronic simple rhinitis · Atrophic rhinitis · **Hypertrophic rhinitis**"* |\n| **Chronic SPECIFIC** | *"Syphilis · T.B. · **Scleroma**"* — the granulomatous trio |\n\n`L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` repeats the specific list — *"Chronic specific: Syphilis · T.B · Rhinoscleroma"* — and spells out the pathology for scleroma: *"**Granulomatous tissue infiltrates the submucosa** of the nose with accumulation of chronic inflammatory cells (plasma cells, lymphocytes and fibroblasts)"*, with CT showing *"expansil granuloma ( scleroma )"* and treatment including *"Surgical excision of nasal granuloma"*.\n\n**Why each of the other three really is granulomatous:**\n\n| Option | Grounding |\n|---|---|\n| **Scleroma** | granulomatous by the deck’s own words, above; Mikulicz cells and Russell bodies are the histological signature (`enthd-nose-372`, `enthd-nose-380`) |\n| **TB** | `L29` lists *"Healed stage of **granulomatous disease. T.B., Scleroma**"* among the causes of secondary atrophic rhinitis — the deck names TB a granulomatous disease outright |\n| **Syphilis** | `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` stages it as *"3ry nodular, **granulomatous**, ulcers, gumma"*; the tertiary gumma is the granuloma, and it is what destroys septal cartilage and bone (`entqb-nose3-117` keys syphilis **and** TB as the infections reaching both cartilage and bone) |\n| **Hypertrophic rhinitis** | ✅ **keyed — the exception.** `L29` calls it an *"Advanced stage of non-specific chronic with **permanent whole mucosa hypertrophy**"*, giving *"Thick, nodular mucosa especially at the posterior end of the inferior turbinate (mulberry like)"*. That is mucosal and fibrous overgrowth, not a granuloma; `entqb-nose3-101` keys the mulberry inferior turbinate to it |\n\n**Bottom line: *specific* in this deck means *granulomatous*.** The three specific infections each end the same way — a healed granuloma leaves the roomy, crusted nose of secondary atrophic rhinitis (`entqb-nose3-108`) — while hypertrophic rhinitis is the opposite process, a nose narrowed by hypertrophy.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Nasal granulomata belong to the chronic SPECIFIC infections — scleroma, TB and syphilis; hypertrophic rhinitis is chronic non-specific mucosal hypertrophy and forms no granuloma.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.103. The two-column classification of chronic rhinitis, the hypertrophic-rhinitis description and *"Healed stage of granulomatous disease. T.B., Scleroma"* are transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the scleroma pathology and the *"Chronic specific"* list from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`; the granulomatous stage of syphilis from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`. Chaptered `ent-rhin` with the corpus’s existing scleroma/TB/syphilis items; **secondary chapter `ent-nasalmass` (granulomatous disease).**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-368', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Which of the following are the **clinical stages of rhinoscleroma**?',
  options:['Atrophic and hypertrophic','Atrophic, hypertrophic and fibrotic','Active, fibrotic and atrophic','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The bank keys the three-stage description — an active (granulomatous) stage, a fibrotic stage and an atrophic stage.**\n\n**⚠️ Record the discrepancy: the cached deck prints FOUR stages, not three, and none of the four menus reproduces its list exactly.** `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` gives them under *"Scleroma- clinical features"*:\n\n| The deck’s stage | The deck’s own gloss |\n|---|---|\n| **Catarrhal** | (named only) — a persistent foul purulent rhinitis |\n| **Hypertrophic** | *"overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue"* |\n| **Atrophic** | *"process of reabsorption and breakdown of tissues"* |\n| **Cicatricial** | *"scar left by the formation of new connective tissue over a healing sore or wound"* |\n\nMapped onto that four-stage list, the keyed option is the standard condensed version: **active** covers the catarrhal and hypertrophic phases, when the granuloma is being laid down; **fibrotic** is the deck’s cicatricial stage under its other name; **atrophic** is unchanged. The key is therefore defensible, but it is not the deck’s wording, and the `answer` stays as printed.\n\n**Why the others are wrong — and why option b is the near-miss:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Atrophic and hypertrophic** | only two of the stages; drops the active/catarrhal onset and the end-stage scar — an incomplete list, not a wrong one |\n| **Atrophic, hypertrophic and fibrotic** | ⚠️ the closest to the deck’s vocabulary (*hypertrophic* is the deck’s word, *fibrotic* stands for *cicatricial*), but it omits the disease’s **beginning** and prints the sequence out of order. **Not the key; recorded here because a student grounding themselves only on `L30` would choose it** |\n| **Active, fibrotic and atrophic** | ✅ **keyed** |\n| **None of the above** | wrong by the key’s own existence |\n\n**Bottom line: whichever naming you use, the disease runs granulomatous overgrowth → resorption → scar**, and the sequence is the clinically important part, because the **cicatricial/fibrotic** end-stage is what produces nasal and subglottic stenosis and drives the surgery (`L30`: *"Surgical excision of nasal granuloma"*). The atrophic stage is asked separately by the endpoint bank — `entep-nose-98` keys *"The nose is roomy with considerable crusts"* — and it is the stage that is clinically indistinguishable from primary atrophic rhinitis.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Rhinoscleroma passes through an active (catarrhal/granulomatous) stage, an atrophic stage of tissue resorption and a fibrotic (cicatricial) scarring stage — the deck names four (catarrhal, hypertrophic, atrophic, cicatricial) and the bank condenses them to three.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.103. Stage list and every gloss transcribed from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`, *"Scleroma- clinical features"*. **⚠️ KEY/DECK DISCREPANCY RECORDED, KEY KEPT: the deck prints FOUR stages — catarrhal, hypertrophic, atrophic, cicatricial — and the keyed trio uses none of *catarrhal*, *hypertrophic* or *cicatricial*. Option b is the option closest to the deck’s vocabulary; the discrepancy is noted in the explanation and the `answer` is left as the bank prints it.** Atrophic-stage cross-reference: `entep-nose-98`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-369', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **pH of the healthy nose**?',
  options:['5.5-6.5 in adults','5-6.7 in adults','5-6.7 in infants','A & C','A & B'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**A & C — the nose is normally slightly ACID, and the printed pair gives the adult range and the infant range, which are not the same range.**\n\n**⚠️ This is a gap in the material and is filled rather than declared.** A grep of every cached ENT lecture for *pH* returns exactly one hit, and it is not nasal: `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf` prints *"PH < 7.2"* as a blood-gas threshold. No cached deck states a nasal pH, and **no other bank in this corpus prints one either** — the numbers below are therefore *(not taken from the course material)*: nasal secretions run **pH 5.5–6.5 in adults** and a slightly wider, slightly more acid **pH 5.0–6.7 in infants**, the infant range being broader because the mucosa and its glandular secretion are still maturing.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **5.5-6.5 in adults** | ✅ correct as printed — the adult range |\n| **5-6.7 in adults** | ✗ the **infant** range mislabelled; the two ranges are deliberately swapped between options a, b and c |\n| **5-6.7 in infants** | ✅ correct as printed — the infant range |\n| **A & C** | ✅ **keyed** — the only option that pairs each range with the right age group |\n| **A & B** | ✗ self-defeating: it asserts **two different adult ranges at once**, and both cannot be the healthy adult pH |\n\n**Why the number matters clinically, and where the deck does connect.** The acid pH is part of the nose’s defence. `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` heads its defence list with the *"Mucociliary system"* and then *"Lysoszymes, opsonins & complement"* and *"Immnoglobulins (secetory IgA, IgE)"* — an enzymic, secretion-dependent barrier whose components work in the mucus, and mucus that is driven off its normal pH loses both its rheology and its ciliary transport *(not taken from the course material)*. That is the mechanism behind the deck’s own therapeutic advice from the other end: `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` recommends *"Saline nasal irrigation: improves mucociliary clearance"*, and `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` uses *"Saline washes"* to reverse rhinitis medicamentosa.\n\n**Bottom line: a healthy nose is mildly acid, and the paediatric range is not the adult one.** Cross-reference `enthd-nose-371` for the factors that govern mucociliary clearance itself.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Healthy nasal secretions are slightly acid — about pH 5.5–6.5 in adults and pH 5.0–6.7 in infants — and the acid pH is part of the nose’s non-specific defence.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.103. **⚠️ GAP FILLED AND TAGGED: no cached ENT deck prints a nasal pH — the only *pH* hit across all lectures is the blood-gas *"PH < 7.2"* in `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf` — and no other bank in the corpus prints the ranges either (a search for 5.5 / 6.5 / 6.7 / *nasal pH* over `questions.ent.js` returns zero). Both ranges are supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged as such in the explanation.** The nasal defence list is from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; saline irrigation from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` and `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-370', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-nasalobs',
  stem:'If the patient **can’t tell whether he has unilateral or bilateral nasal obstruction**, we can make sure through which of the following tests?',
  options:['Cottle test','Cotton test','Olfaction test','All of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The cotton test — a wisp of cotton wool held at one nostril at a time, watched for movement with the other side occluded. It is the only one of the three that measures AIRFLOW SIDE BY SIDE.**\n\n**Why the Cottle test cannot answer this question, on the deck’s own words.** `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` names it once and ties it to a single structure: *"Deviations in region of **nasal valve** cause greatest obstruction : cottle test"*. It is performed by drawing the cheek laterally to pull the lateral nasal wall open; a positive test tells you the obstruction is at the **valve**, and Grade Gain keys exactly that — `entqb-nose8-245` asks what a positive Cottle test indicates and keys *"Nasal valve collapse"*. It answers **where**, never **which side**.\n\n**⚠️ The cotton test itself is not printed by any cached deck and is supplied here** *(not taken from the course material)*: a shred of cotton wool or a cold metal spatula is held under each nostril in turn while the other is gently occluded, and the side that fails to move the cotton — or fails to mist the spatula — is the obstructed one. What the deck does print is the clinical need for it: `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` classifies obstruction *"According to laterality"* into unilateral (*"usually points to structural abnormality or unilateral pathology"*), bilateral, and *"**Alternating** nasal obstruction… a variety of bilateral nasal obstruction… frequently position-related"*, and its assessment slide separates the **subjective** — *"History taking (**laterality**, duration, other symptoms)"* — from the **objective**, which is *"Clinical examination and nasal endoscopy"*, with *"Rhinomanometry and acoustic rhinometry (mostly of academic interest)"*. This stem is precisely the case where the subjective route has failed, so an objective bedside check is needed.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Cottle test** | ✗ localises obstruction to the **nasal valve**; says nothing about laterality (`entqb-nose8-245`) |\n| **Cotton test** | ✅ **keyed** — airflow, one side at a time |\n| **Olfaction test** | ✗ measures **smell**, a sensory function; conductive anosmia tells you air is not reaching the olfactory cleft but not on which side |\n| **All of the above** | ✗ fails because two of the three do not test laterality at all |\n\n**Bottom line: laterality is an airflow question, so use an airflow test.** Why laterality is worth establishing: the deck’s rule is that **unilateral** obstruction is structural or unilateral pathology, **fluctuating and bilateral** obstruction is mucosal.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The cotton (cotton-wool/spatula) test compares airflow through each nostril and settles whether nasal obstruction is unilateral or bilateral; the Cottle test localises obstruction to the nasal valve and tests nothing about side.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.103. *"Deviations in region of nasal valve cause greatest obstruction : cottle test"* is transcribed from `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`; the laterality classification and the subjective/objective assessment lists from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; the Cottle-positive meaning is Grade Gain’s keyed `entqb-nose8-245`. **⚠️ GAP FILLED AND TAGGED: the cotton-wool/spatula test itself is printed by no cached ENT deck and by no other bank in the corpus (*Cotton* hits in `questions.ent.js` are all cotton soaked with adrenaline for epistaxis); the technique is supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.** ⚠️ *Cottle* and *Cotton* differ by one letter and are two different tests — not a printing error.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-371', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'**Mucociliary clearance** mechanism depends on all of the following **except**?',
  options:['Amount of mucus','Patency of air current','Temperature of the inhaled air','Anatomic factors affecting nasal airflow'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The temperature of the inhaled air — because the nose conditions the air BEFORE it reaches the ciliated field, so what arrives at the mucus blanket is body-temperature saturated air whatever the weather outside.**\n\n**How important the mechanism is, in the deck’s own ranking.** `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` heads its list of nasal *"Defence- Mechanisms"* with *"1. **Mucociliary system**"*, ticked, above epithelial integrity, reflexes, lysozymes and immunoglobulins — and Grade Gain goes further, keying mucociliary clearance as **the main function of the nose** outright (`entqb-nose1-28`, cross-referenced by `enthd-nose-350`). `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` supplies the two halves of the machine: the respiratory area is *"Lined by pseudo stratified columnar **ciliated** with **goblet cells**"* — cilia to move it, goblet cells to make it.\n\n| Option | Why the deck makes it a determinant |\n|---|---|\n| **Amount of mucus** | the goblet-cell product is the blanket the cilia push. Too little and it dries to crusts — `L29`’s atrophic rhinitis has *"scanty viscid secretions that rapidly dries to form crusts"*; too much or too stagnant and it sits — `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` lists *"**Stagnation of nasal secretions**"* as its own cause of mucopurulent discharge |\n| **Patency of air current** | a blocked channel cannot be cleared. `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` treats it directly: *"Saline nasal irrigation: **improves mucociliary clearance**"* |\n| **Anatomic factors affecting nasal airflow** | `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` lists *"**Anatomical variations**"* among the predisposing factors of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis, and `L29`’s atrophic-rhinitis CT records *"Loss of definition of the ostiomeatal complex"* — the anatomy is the drainage route |\n| **Temperature of the inhaled air** | ✅ **keyed — the exception.** `L23` gives the nose the job of *"Air way, purification of air"*; conditioning inspired air is a nasal **function**, not an input to clearance |\n\n**⚠️ The key is defensible rather than absolute, and that is worth recording.** Ciliary beat frequency is genuinely temperature-sensitive, and prolonged cold dry air does slow mucociliary transport *(not taken from the course material)*; what makes the keyed option the odd one out is that the nose normalises the air first, so temperature at the nostril is not one of the mechanism’s own variables. The deck’s own list of what impairs cilia never mentions temperature — `L27.1` names *"Viral Infection · Smoking · Allergy · Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia"*. The `answer` is left exactly as the bank prints it.\n\n**Bottom line: mucus, cilia and a patent anatomical route — three things; air temperature is what the nose fixes, not what fixes the nose.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Mucociliary clearance depends on the quantity and quality of mucus, on the patency of the airway and on the anatomy of nasal airflow; the temperature of inspired air is conditioned by the nose itself and is not a determinant of clearance.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.103. *"Defence- Mechanisms… 1. Mucociliary system"*, the atrophic crusting and the ostiomeatal CT line are from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the ciliated/goblet lining and *"Air way, purification of air"* from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; *"Anatomical variations"* and the four causes of ciliary impairment from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; *"Saline nasal irrigation: improves mucociliary clearance"* from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; *"Stagnation of nasal secretions"* from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; clearance as the main nasal function is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose1-28`. **⚠️ ARGUABLE KEY RECORDED, KEY KEPT: no cached deck lists the determinants of mucociliary clearance as a set, and ciliary beat frequency is in fact temperature-dependent — the outside-knowledge counter-argument is tagged in the explanation and the printed key is retained.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-372', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'What do **"Russell bodies"** in the histopathology of **nasal scleroma** represent?',
  options:['Hematoxylin-stained degenerated plasma cells','Eosin-stained degenerated plasma cells','Plasma cells filled with inclusion bodies','Histiocytes that appear as vacuolated foam cells'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Eosin-stained degenerated plasma cells — the lecture defines them in almost exactly those words.**\n\n`L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`, under *"Scleroma- pathology"*, prints the two cells of the disease side by side:\n\n> *"The infiltration is charecterized by the presence of: **Miculicz cells** (large foam cells containing the causative bacilli within its vacuoles) · **Russel bodies** (degenerated plasma cells with an **eosinophil staining cytoplasm**)"*\n\n*Eosinophil staining cytoplasm* means it takes up **eosin**, the pink counterstain of the H&E pair — so *eosin-stained* is the same statement in the question’s vocabulary. The background is the deck’s own account of the infiltrate: *"Granulomatous tissue infiltrates the submucosa of the nose with accumulation of chronic inflammatory cells (**plasma cells**, lymphocytes and fibroblasts)"* — plasma cells are the raw material, and Russell bodies are what they become as they degenerate.\n\n| Option | What it actually describes | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Hematoxylin-stained degenerated plasma cells** | haematoxylin is the **blue basophilic** half of H&E and stains **nuclei**; the deck specifies the **cytoplasm** and specifies eosinophilic — the colour is inverted | ✗ |\n| **Eosin-stained degenerated plasma cells** | the deck’s definition, restated | ✅ **keyed** |\n| **Plasma cells filled with inclusion bodies** | ⚠️ this is the **Mott cell**, and Grade Gain keys it as such: `entqb-nose3-121` asks what lies *"in the intermediate stage between plasma cells and Russel bodies"* and keys **Mott cells**. A close and deliberate trap — strictly, the Russell body is the eosinophilic immunoglobulin **inclusion**, and a plasma cell stuffed with those inclusions is a Mott cell; **this bank follows the deck, which names the degenerated CELL itself** | ✗ |\n| **Histiocytes that appear as vacuolated foam cells** | the **Mikulicz cell** — the deck’s *"large foam cells containing the causative bacilli within its vacuoles"*, keyed pathognomonic of scleroma by `entqb-nose3-120` and asked again in this bank as `enthd-nose-380` | ✗ |\n\n**Bottom line: two cells, two jobs — the Mikulicz cell carries the organism, the Russell body is the exhausted plasma cell.** Neither is unique to the nose: the same histology defines laryngoscleroma in the subglottis (`entqb-thr9-454`, `entep-throat-69`).\n\n**Note as printed:** the deck spells them *"Russel bodies"* and *"Miculicz cells"*; the accepted spellings are **Russell** and **Mikulicz**, which is what this bank prints.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Russell bodies in scleroma are degenerated plasma cells with eosinophilic (eosin-stained) cytoplasm; Mott cells are the intermediate plasma cell packed with inclusions, and Mikulicz cells are the vacuolated foam cells carrying the bacilli.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.104. Both cell definitions and the composition of the infiltrate transcribed verbatim from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`, *"Scleroma- pathology"*. Mott cells as the intermediate stage is Grade Gain’s keyed `entqb-nose3-121`; Mikulicz as pathognomonic is `entqb-nose3-120`. **Deck spellings *"Russel"* / *"Miculicz"* noted once in the explanation; the bank prints the accepted spellings.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-373', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Which of the following describes the term **"Perennial nasal allergy"**?',
  options:['Seasonal nasal allergy','Nasal allergy that is found all the year round','Nasal allergy resistant to treatment','Allergy that affects multiple organs'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Perennial means year-round — the word is doing all the work, and it is defined against *seasonal*, not against severity.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` sets the two terms out as the allergen-based classification of allergic rhinitis, listing under *"Allergens"*: *"Seasonal rhinitis · **Perennial allergic rhinitis** · Occupational allergens · Food and drug induced rhinitis · Role of pollution"*. The deck does not gloss the word *(the year-round definition and the allergen split below are therefore **not taken from the course material**)*: **seasonal** disease follows an airborne allergen with a season — the deck illustrates it with *"Grass Pollen"*, and Grade Gain keys **pollen** as the cause of intermittent allergic rhinitis (`entqb-nose3-129`) — whereas **perennial** disease follows allergens that are present indoors all year, chiefly **house dust mite**, animal dander, moulds and cockroach. Same IgE mechanism, different exposure calendar. This bank asks the companion question directly at `enthd-nose-360`.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Seasonal nasal allergy** | ✗ the **opposite** term of the same pair — symptoms confined to the allergen’s season |\n| **Nasal allergy that is found all the year round** | ✅ **keyed** |\n| **Nasal allergy resistant to treatment** | ✗ *perennial* is about **duration of exposure**, not response to therapy. Persistent symptoms despite treatment raise different questions — nasal polyposis, Samter’s triad (`enthd-nose-364`), or a non-allergic diagnosis such as NARES, which `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` defines as *"Allergic-like rhinitis with eosinophilia in nasal secretions… **Allergy testing is typically negative**"* |\n| **Allergy that affects multiple organs** | ✗ that is **atopy**. The deck does record the multi-organ links — *"Allergic rhinitis and bronchial asthma frequently coexist"*, and *"Allergic rhinitis with diffuse sinonasal polyposis + asprin hypersensitivity and brnchial asthma → (Samtter’s Triad )"* — but none of that is what *perennial* means |\n\n**⚠️ A dated term, and the deck says so by what it prints next.** Immediately after the allergen list, `L29` gives the current **ARIA** classification, which replaced *seasonal/perennial* with **intermittent** (*"< 4 days/week Or < 4 weeks Consecutive/y"*) versus **persistent** (*"> 4 days /week & > 4 weeks Consecutive /y"*), graded **mild** or **moderate-to-severe** on sleep, daily activity and work. The reason for the change is that a perennial allergen can cause intermittent symptoms and a seasonal one persistent symptoms; the newer axis describes the **patient**, the older one the **allergen**. Definition cross-references: `enthd-nose-366`, `entqb-nose3-88`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Perennial allergic rhinitis is allergic rhinitis present all year round, driven by year-round indoor allergens, as opposed to seasonal disease; the modern ARIA axis of intermittent versus persistent has largely replaced the pair.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.104. The seasonal/perennial allergen list, the *"Grass Pollen"* illustration, the ARIA intermittent/persistent thresholds, the asthma coexistence and Samter’s triad are transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the NARES definition from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`; pollen as the intermittent allergen is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose3-129`. **⚠️ GAP FILLED AND TAGGED: the deck names *perennial* but never defines it — the year-round definition and the indoor-allergen list are supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-374', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'**Velvet appearance of the nasal turbinates** is characteristic of which of the following conditions?',
  options:['Atrophic rhinitis','Hypertrophic rhinitis','Allergic rhinitis','Vasomotor rhinitis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Hypertrophic rhinitis — the turbinate mucosa is permanently thickened, so its surface goes granular and velvety, and at the posterior end of the inferior turbinate it becomes frankly nodular.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` describes it in two lines that are the whole answer: chronic hypertrophic rhinitis is an *"Advanced stage of non-specific chronic with **permanent whole mucosa hypertrophy**"*, showing *"**Thick, nodular mucosa** especially at the **posterior end of the inferior turbinate (mulberry like)**"*, with *"Sometimes polyps may form"* and *"+/_ Rhinitis medicamentosa"*. Grade Gain keys the same sign to the same disease: `entqb-nose3-101` asks where mulberry-like inferior turbinate mucosa is found and keys **chronic hypertrophic rhinitis**.\n\n**⚠️ The word *velvet* is printed by no cached ENT deck** — a grep of all lectures returns zero — so the descriptor itself is supplied *(not taken from the course material)*: a thickened, granular, matte mucosal surface that has lost its normal wet sheen, the earlier and finer-grained version of the same change the deck calls *mulberry* once it is coarse and nodular.\n\n| Option | The deck’s own picture of the turbinates | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Atrophic rhinitis** | the **opposite**: *"progressive atrophy of nasal mucosa and the underlying bone of the turbinates"*, *"**abnormal patency**"*, crusts, *"Atrophy of inferior & middle turbinates"* — a shrunken, crusted, roomy nose | ✗ |\n| **Hypertrophic rhinitis** | *"permanent whole mucosa hypertrophy"*, thick and nodular | ✅ **keyed** |\n| **Allergic rhinitis** | `L29` gives it a different surface and a different colour: *"**Pale bluish edematous** nasal turbinates"* — swollen with oedema fluid, smooth and boggy, not thickened tissue | ✗ |\n| **Vasomotor rhinitis** | `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`: *"results from changes in **vascular tone**"* and *"Symptoms of vasomotor rhinitis are similar to those of allergic rhinitis"* — vascular engorgement that comes and goes, so no fixed surface change | ✗ |\n\n**⚠️ Recorded, key kept: the sign is characteristic rather than exclusive.** Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose9-260` keys the cobblestone/mulberry appearance as occurring in allergic rhinitis, chronic rhinosinusitis and vasomotor rhinitis, and **absent only in atrophic rhinitis** — so a mulberry surface alone does not name the disease. What separates them here is the deck’s wording: allergic and vasomotor turbinates are **oedematous and reversible**, hypertrophic turbinates are **permanently hypertrophied**.\n\n**Bottom line: shrunken = atrophic, pale bluish and boggy = allergic, fluctuating = vasomotor, thick and velvety-to-mulberry = hypertrophic** — and the last one is the one that stops responding to decongestants and ends in turbinate surgery (`entqb-nose3-110` keys topical steroids as the *initial* treatment first).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A thickened, velvety-to-mulberry turbinate surface, worst at the posterior end of the inferior turbinate, is the sign of chronic hypertrophic rhinitis; allergic turbinates are pale bluish and oedematous and atrophic turbinates are shrunken and crusted.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.104. The hypertrophic, allergic and atrophic turbinate descriptions are transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the vasomotor mechanism from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`; mulberry-to-hypertrophic is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose3-101`, initial treatment `entqb-nose3-110`. **⚠️ *Velvet* appears in NO cached ENT lecture (grep returns zero) — the descriptor is supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation. ⚠️ Non-exclusivity recorded, key kept: `entqb-nose9-260` keys the mulberry/cobblestone surface as present in allergic, chronic rhinosinusitic and vasomotor mucosa too.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-375', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most specific diagnostic test for allergic rhinitis**?',
  options:['Nasal smear eosinophilia','Skin tests','RAST','All of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**RAST — the specific-IgE blood test. It is the only one of the three that identifies the antibody to a named allergen, and the deck disqualifies the other two in its own words.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` runs three investigation slides and demolishes two of the options on the way:\n\n| Investigation | The deck’s verdict, verbatim |\n|---|---|\n| **Eosinophils** (blood sample / nasal smear) | *"**Only supports** the diagnosis of allergic rhinitis but it is **neither sensitive or specific**"* — and it names two false-positive sources beside it: *"Non-allergic eosinophilic rhinitis"* and *"Parasitic infection"* |\n| **Total IgE** | *"About **50 %** of patients with allergic rhinitis have **normal** levels of total IgE and **20 %** of non-affected individuals can have **elevated** total IgE levels"* — a test that misses half the disease and flags a fifth of the healthy |\n| **Specific IgE (RAST test)** | listed as the second blood test, and left standing |\n| **Skin allergy test** | *"An allergen extract is placed into skin by either scratching or pricking with a sharp device or by intradermal injection using a syringe & needle"* — described in full, but the deck makes no claim about its specificity |\n\n**The other bank prints the ranking outright and is preferred here to outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose3-96` asks for the *most diagnostic* test for allergic rhinitis on a menu of nasal smear eosinophils, CBC, total IgE and specific IgE, and keys ***Specific IgE blood test (RAST)***. Two banks, same key.\n\n| Option | Why it is not the most specific |\n|---|---|\n| **Nasal smear eosinophilia** | ✗ excluded by the deck itself; NARES gives the same smear with **negative allergy testing** (`L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`), and so does a worm |\n| **Skin tests** | ✗ highly **sensitive** and the usual first-line screen, but they read the patient’s **skin mast cells**, not the antibody: antihistamines suppress them, dermographism and eczema confound them, and a wheal can mark sensitisation without clinical disease *(these specific caveats are **not taken from the course material**)* |\n| **RAST** | ✅ **keyed** — measures circulating IgE against a defined allergen, unaffected by drugs or skin disease |\n| **All of the above** | ✗ impossible once the deck has called eosinophilia *"neither sensitive or specific"* |\n\n**Bottom line: sensitivity and specificity are not the same question.** Skin tests find who to suspect; RAST proves which allergen. Neither replaces the history — the diagnosis in `L29` is clinical, *"Sneezing, itching, watery rhinorrhea and a sensation of nasal obstruction"* in an atopic exposed to *"common aero-allergens"* — and the tests exist mainly to direct **avoidance** and **immunotherapy**, the first and third arms of the deck’s management triad.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'RAST (allergen-specific IgE) is the most specific test for allergic rhinitis; nasal smear eosinophilia is explicitly neither sensitive nor specific, and total IgE is normal in half of sufferers and raised in a fifth of normals.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.104. Every investigation verdict — the eosinophil line, the 50 %/20 % total-IgE figures, the skin-test technique and specific IgE (RAST) — is transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; NARES with negative allergy testing from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`. **The ranking itself is taken from Grade Gain’s keyed `entqb-nose3-96` in preference to outside knowledge; the skin-test caveats (antihistamine suppression, dermographism, sensitisation without disease) are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-376', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'**Treatment of common cold** includes all of the following **except**?',
  options:['Warm fluids','Bed rest','Antibiotics','Analgesics'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Antibiotics — the common cold is a viral illness that resolves on its own, so every other option is supportive care and this one treats nothing that is there.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` files it under *"Infective **viral** Rhinosinusitis"*, calls it *"The commenst viral infection in human"*, names **rhinovirus** and **coronavirus** as the agents, traces the illness to *"Release of mediators from infected cells e.g. IL-8, kinins, prostaglandins"* — and gives the fact that settles the question: *"**Resolution within 5-10 days**"*. Grade Gain keys the virology from the other side: `entqb-nose3-97` asks which organism does **not** cause the common cold and keys **influenza virus**, leaving rhino-, corona- and adenovirus.\n\n**⚠️ RECORD THE DISCREPANCY: THE DECK’S OWN TREATMENT SLIDE PRINTS ANTIBIOTICS.** `L29`’s *"Treatment"* list for the common cold reads, in full: *"Bed rest. · Vitamins. · Fluids. · Analgesic. · Antipyretics. · Antihistaminics. · Anticholenergic. · Decongestant. · **Antibiotics.**"* — so the lecture lists **all four options**, and taken literally the deck supports no exception at all. The `answer` is kept exactly as the bank prints it, and the reconciliation is this: the slide immediately before that list is the **complications** slide — *"Sinusitis. · Pharyngitis. · Adenoiditis. · Tonsillitis. · Otitis media. · Laryngotrachitis. · Bronchitis. · Pneumonia. · Nephritis. · Rheumatic fever."* Antibiotics belong to those **bacterial sequelae**, not to the cold itself, and the deck’s teaching everywhere else is antibiotic restraint: `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` makes resistance its central treatment theme, warning of *"Bacterial production of enzymes such as beta-lactamase that"* destroy the antibiotic.\n\n| Option | Deck support | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Warm fluids** | *"Fluids."* — hydration thins secretions and replaces insensible loss in a febrile patient | supportive |\n| **Bed rest** | *"Bed rest."*, printed first | supportive |\n| **Antibiotics** | printed, but with **no bacterial target**: the deck’s own agents are viruses and the illness resolves in 5-10 days | ✅ **keyed — the exception** |\n| **Analgesics** | *"Analgesic."* and *"Antipyretics."*, for the deck’s *"Fever, headache, malaise… Sore throat"* | supportive |\n\n**Bottom line: treat the symptoms, wait out the virus, and reserve the antibiotic for the complication.** The practical trigger for changing your mind is the timeline this bank teaches elsewhere — a cold that is still worsening beyond **10 days**, or that improves and then worsens again, has become acute **bacterial** rhinosinusitis (`enthd-nose-348`), and that is when an antibiotic starts to have a target.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The common cold is viral and self-limiting within 5–10 days, so treatment is supportive — rest, fluids, analgesics and antipyretics — and antibiotics are reserved for bacterial complications.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.104. Classification as viral, the agents, the mediator list, *"Resolution within 5-10 days"*, and the complication and treatment lists are all transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the β-lactamase line from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the non-influenza virology is Grade Gain’s keyed `entqb-nose3-97`. **⚠️⚠️ KEY/DECK DISCREPANCY RECORDED, KEY KEPT: `L29`’s common-cold treatment slide EXPLICITLY LISTS *"Antibiotics."* alongside bed rest, fluids and analgesics — the deck therefore prints all four options as treatment and supports no exception. The key is defensible only against the deck’s own complications slide, and that reconciliation is set out in the explanation; the `answer` is left as the bank prints it.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-377', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'All of the following are among the **characteristics of atrophic rhinitis** **except**?',
  options:['Roomy nasal cavity','Anosmia','Offensive odor','Crustations','None of the above'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**None of the above — all four are genuine features, so there is no exception to find.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` defines the disease in a single sentence that contains three of the four outright: *"Chronic nasal disease characterized by progressive **atrophy of nasal mucosa and the underlying bone of the turbinates** with formation of scanty viscid secretions that rapidly dries to form **crusts** that emit a characteristic **foul odor (ozaena)**. There is associated **abnormal patency of nasal passages**."*\n\n| Feature | Where the deck prints it |\n|---|---|\n| **Roomy nasal cavity** | *"abnormal patency of nasal passages"*; the CT slide adds *"**Enlargement of the nasal cavities** with erosion and bowing of the lateral nasal wall"* and *"Bony resorption and mucosal atrophy of the inferior and middle turbinates"* |\n| **Offensive odor** | *"foul odor (**ozaena**)"* in the definition, and *"**Fetor oris**"* heads the signs. `entqb-nose3-103` keys fetor oris/ozaena to atrophic rhinitis |\n| **Crustations** | *"**Green, yellow or black crusts**"* among the signs, with *"Ulcerated bleeding mucosa"* beside them — which is why the symptom list includes **epistaxis** |\n| **Anosmia** | ⚠️ **not in `L29`’s symptom list**, which prints *"Nasal obstruction. Headache. Epistaxis. Bad odor from the nose. Chocking."* **The other bank supplies it and is preferred to outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose3-102` asks which smell disorder a patient with atrophic rhinitis is most likely to experience and keys **anosmia** |\n\n**Why anosmia is the feature the stem is really testing.** The olfactory epithelium sits high, *"related to the superior turbinate, cribriform plate"* (`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`), and atrophic rhinitis destroys mucosa and buries the cleft under crust — so the loss is both conductive and, as the receptor field atrophies, sensorineural. It produces the disease’s cruellest paradox: **the patient cannot smell the ozaena that everyone around them can** *(the paradox itself is **not taken from the course material**)*. `L25.2) smell.pdf` supports the mechanism generically, listing *"Physical obstacle of odorant to reach olfactory epithelium"* and *"Rheological properties of mucus"* as conductive causes, and *"Impairment of olfactory epithelium function"* as sensorineural.\n\n**Bottom line: a wide, dry, crusted, stinking nose that feels blocked and cannot smell.** The paradox of obstruction in an abnormally patent cavity is the diagnostic giveaway; treatment in the deck is only *"irrigations · humidification to provide moisture · experimental surgical procedures aiming at **narrowing** the nasal cavity with submucosal implants"*. Cross-references: `enthd-nose-383` (secondary causes), `entep-nose-98` (the atrophic stage of scleroma, clinically identical).\n\n**Note as printed:** the bank spells option d *"Crustations"*; the usual form is **crusting/crusts**, which is what `L29` writes. Kept as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Atrophic rhinitis is characterised by an abnormally roomy nasal cavity, foul-smelling crusts (ozaena) and anosmia — all four printed options are true features, so the answer is none of the above.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.105. The definition, symptom and sign lists, CT findings and treatment options are transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the olfactory-area location from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; the conductive/sensorineural mechanism from `L25.2) smell.pdf`. **⚠️ `L29` does NOT list anosmia among atrophic rhinitis’s symptoms — it is taken from Grade Gain’s keyed `entqb-nose3-102` in preference to outside knowledge; fetor oris cross-checked at `entqb-nose3-103`. The merciful-anosmia paradox is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.** Printed spelling *"Crustations"* kept and noted once.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-379', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Which of the following best demonstrates the term **"rhinitis medicamentosa"**?',
  options:["It's due to abuse of topical nasal decongestants","It causes fiery red edematous nasal mucosa","It occurs as a result of 2ry vasodilatation after the effects of decongestion wear off",'All of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All of the above — one cause, one mechanism, one appearance, and the bank is asking you to hold all three at once.**\n\n**a. The cause is printed twice in the decks.** `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` files it among the non-allergic non-infective rhinitides and gives it in one line: *"**Etiology: Prolonged excessive use nasal decongestants** ‘rebound congestion (rebound phenomenon)’ · Saline washes and topical steroids can help to reverse these changes."* `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` turns the same fact into a prescribing rule: topical decongestant drops for *"**not more than 5 days** to avoid development of rhinitis medicamentosa (drug-induced rhinitis)"*. The endpoint bank keys the identical statement — `entep-nose-63` keys *"Is due to prolonged use of topical nasal decongestants"*.\n\n**c. The mechanism is the deck’s own *rebound phenomenon*, spelled out.** Topical α-agonists work by constricting the venous sinusoids of the turbinates; with repeated dosing the receptors down-regulate and the vessels dilate again as each dose wears off — **secondary vasodilatation** — so the nose is more blocked than before and the patient reaches for the bottle again. The deck names the phenomenon but not the receptor mechanism *(the down-regulation detail is **not taken from the course material**)*. `L31` adds why the oral route is not a way round it: pseudoephedrine and phenylephrine are *"contraindicated in hypertension, hypertrophy of the prostate, and glaucoma"*.\n\n**b. The appearance.** ⚠️ **The phrase *fiery red* is printed by no cached ENT deck** (grep returns zero), so the description is supplied *(not taken from the course material)*: a beefy red, boggy, oedematous mucosa that has lost its response to further decongestant — the visual opposite of the *"Pale bluish edematous nasal turbinates"* that `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` gives allergic rhinitis, and distinct from the *"Purple mucosa (venous stasis)"* it gives simple chronic rhinitis. The nearest thing the decks print is `L29`’s *"**Red mucosa (active infection)**"* for simple chronic rhinitis, which is a different cause of the same colour.\n\n**Why *All of the above* is the only defensible key:** each single option is true but partial, and the three are not alternatives — they are cause, mechanism and sign of one entity. Rejecting any one of them would require the decks to contradict it, and none does.\n\n**Bottom line: five days is the whole prophylaxis.** Treatment is to **stop the drops** and cover the rebound with *"Saline washes and topical steroids"* (`L30`). Note the trap for later: `L29` lists *"+/_ Rhinitis medicamentosa"* as a feature of chronic **hypertrophic** rhinitis — the two coexist, and the chronically abused nose ends up permanently hypertrophied (`enthd-nose-374`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Rhinitis medicamentosa is rebound congestion from prolonged topical decongestant abuse — secondary vasodilatation as each dose wears off — producing a red oedematous mucosa; topical drops are therefore limited to five days.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.105. The aetiology, rebound phenomenon and saline/steroid reversal are transcribed from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`; the five-day rule and the oral-decongestant contraindications from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; the mucosal colours of allergic, simple chronic and hypertrophic rhinitis from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the decongestant-abuse aetiology is also keyed by `entep-nose-63`. **⚠️ GAP FILLED AND TAGGED: *fiery red* appears in NO cached ENT lecture (grep returns zero), and the α-receptor down-regulation mechanism is likewise unprinted — both are supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.** Options a and c are kept exactly as the bank prints them, *"It’s"* included.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-380', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Which of the following cells is the **active cell of scleroma**?',
  options:['Russell bodies','Mott cells','Mikulicz cells','Fibroblasts'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The Mikulicz cell — it is the cell that is CARRYING THE LIVE ORGANISM, which is what makes it the active one.**\n\n`L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` defines it under *"Scleroma- pathology"*: *"**Miculicz cells** (large foam cells containing the **causative bacilli within its vacuoles**)"*. The bacillus is *"**Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis** ( gram negative )"*, and the whole diagnostic strategy follows from where it lives — *"Diagnosis : **Biopsy** . Culture and sensitivity test"*, treated by *"long course of antibiotics ( aminoglycosides , Tetracyclin )+/- systemic steroids"* plus *"Surgical excision of nasal granuloma"*. A long course is needed precisely because the organism is **intracellular**, sheltered inside those vacuoles *(the intracellular-sanctuary rationale is **not taken from the course material**)*. Grade Gain keys the same cell as **pathognomonic** of the disease (`entqb-nose3-120`).\n\n| Option | What it is | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Russell bodies** | the deck’s *"degenerated plasma cells with an eosinophil staining cytoplasm"* — an **end-product**, the burnt-out debris of the immune response, carrying no organism. Full table at `enthd-nose-372` | ✗ |\n| **Mott cells** | the plasma cell stuffed with immunoglobulin inclusions, keyed by `entqb-nose3-121` as *"the intermediate stage between plasma cells and Russel bodies"* — a stage on the way to that debris, and **printed by no cached deck** | ✗ |\n| **Mikulicz cells** | ✅ **keyed** | |\n| **Fibroblasts** | genuinely present — the deck’s infiltrate is *"plasma cells, lymphocytes and **fibroblasts**"* — and they are the cells that build the *"overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue"* of the hypertrophic stage and the *"scar… of new connective tissue"* of the cicatricial stage. But that is the **host’s scarring response**, the disease’s consequence rather than its engine | ✗ |\n\n**Bottom line: three cells, one question each — which cell holds the bacillus (Mikulicz), which cell is the spent antibody factory (Russell body, via Mott cell), and which cell lays the scar (fibroblast).** Only the first answers *active*. The same histology defines the disease wherever it sits: laryngoscleroma takes the **subglottis** (`entep-throat-69`), and `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf` lists *"Laryngeal granulomas (Scleroma)"* among the causes of adult stridor.\n\n**Note as printed:** the deck spells it *"Miculicz"*; the accepted spelling is **Mikulicz**, which this bank prints.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The Mikulicz cell — a large vacuolated foam cell holding Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis inside its vacuoles — is the active, pathognomonic cell of scleroma; Russell bodies and Mott cells are spent plasma cells and fibroblasts build the scar.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.105. Cell definitions, organism, infiltrate composition, stage descriptions, diagnosis and treatment all transcribed from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`; adult stridor from scleroma from `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf`. Mikulicz as pathognomonic is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose3-120`; Mott cells as the intermediate stage `entqb-nose3-121` — **the deck prints no Mott cell at all, so that option is grounded on the other bank in preference to outside knowledge.** The intracellular rationale for a long antibiotic course is tagged in the explanation. Shared cell table lives at `enthd-nose-372`; deck spelling *"Miculicz"* noted once.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-381', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'**Hebra nose** is characteristic of which of the following disorders?',
  options:["Wegner's granuloma",'Scleroma',"Stewart's granuloma",'Sarcoidosis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Scleroma — the Hebra nose is the broadened, flattened, woody nose left by the CICATRICIAL stage, when the granuloma has healed into scar.**\n\n**⚠️ The eponym is printed by no cached ENT deck** — a grep of every lecture for *Hebra* returns zero — **so it is taken from the other bank rather than from outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose3-119` asks what causes the Hebra nose on a menu of syphilis, tuberculosis, scleroma and chronic rhinosinusitis, and keys **scleroma**. Two banks, same key. What `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` does print is the process behind the deformity: scleroma runs *"Catarrhal · Hypertrophic: **overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue** · Atrophic · Cicatricial: **scar left by the formation of new connective tissue** over a healing sore or wound"*, and CT shows *"expansil granuloma"*. Fibrous overgrowth first, contracting scar second — the external nose is splayed and hardened, and the vestibule stenoses. *(That the eponym honours Ferdinand von Hebra is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n| Option | The deck’s own nasal picture | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Wegner’s granuloma** | *"Autoimmune collagen disorders with multisystem involvement… necrotizing vasculitis… **Ulceration of the septum with crusting and saddle nose** are common nasal presentations"*; diagnosed on *"increased serum c ANCA , ESR , BIOPSY"*. Its deformity is the **saddle** — a collapsed dorsum, not a broadened one (`entqb-nose6-191`) | ✗ |\n| **Scleroma** | ✅ **keyed** | |\n| **Stewart’s granuloma** | *"Midline lethal granuloma… A rare **locally destructive** lesion · Radiotherapy and chemotherapy may halt the process · **Most cases progress rapidly to death**"* — it destroys the midface rather than scarring it into a shape | ✗ |\n| **Sarcoidosis** | *"the mucosa becomes swollen and granular, dry, erythematous, and with a **strawberry-like appearance**. Crusting and stenosis"*, with *"**ACE** levels in serum… elevated"* — its eponymous appearance is the **strawberry mucosa**, an internal sign | ✗ |\n\n**Bottom line: three named appearances, three diseases — Hebra nose = scleroma, saddle nose = Wegener’s (also syphilis and neglected septal haematoma, `entqb-nose6-191`), strawberry mucosa = sarcoidosis.** Note the fourth in the family: `entqb-nose3-116` keys the **shrunken bridge deformity** to **syphilis**.\n\n**⚠️ Notes as printed. (1) Option a is printed *"Wegner’s granuloma"*; the correct spelling is **Wegener’s** (granulomatosis with polyangiitis) — kept as printed here, and the decks misspell it the same way (`L30`: *"Mnagment of wagner’s granulomatosis"*; `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`: *"ANCA (Wegner’s granulomatosis)"*). (2) This exact question, with the same four options in the same order and the same key, is printed AGAIN as Q382 on the next page of the book — a verbatim double-print, folded to this single entry.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The Hebra nose — a broadened, flattened, indurated external nose — is the cicatricial deformity of rhinoscleroma; Wegener’s gives a saddle nose and sarcoidosis a strawberry mucosa.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.105. **⚠️ VERBATIM DOUBLE-PRINT: the identical question, options and key are printed again as Q382 on p.106; Q382 is folded and this is the single retained entry.** The scleroma stages, and the Wegener’s, Stewart’s and sarcoidosis descriptions, are transcribed from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`; the ANCA spelling from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`. **⚠️ *Hebra* appears in NO cached ENT lecture (grep returns zero) — the eponym is taken from Grade Gain’s keyed `entqb-nose3-119` in preference to outside knowledge; saddle nose `entqb-nose6-191`, shrunken bridge `entqb-nose3-116`. ⚠️ Option a is printed *"Wegner’s granuloma"* for **Wegener’s** and is kept as printed; the decks share the misspelling.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-383', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'All of the following are **causes of 2ry atrophic rhinitis** **except**?',
  options:['Chemotherapy','Excessive surgical resection of inferior turbinates','Deviated nasal septum','Scleroma'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Chemotherapy — the deck’s secondary list contains RADIOTHERAPY, and swapping one for the other is the whole trick of the question.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` prints the secondary aetiology as four numbered items:\n\n> *"1. **Excessive surgical destruction of nasal mucosa.** 2. Chronic exposure to irritant, cocaine or toxic agents 3. **Healed stage of granulomatous disease. T.B., Scleroma.** 4. **Radiotherapy.**"*\n\n| Option | Grounding | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Chemotherapy** | ✅ **keyed — the exception.** Absent from the deck’s list, and mechanistically different: cytotoxics cause a **generalised, reversible mucositis**, whereas the atrophic nose is built by **permanent** loss of mucosa, glands and turbinate bone. Radiotherapy does that because it is local and fibrosing *(the mucositis-versus-fibrosis contrast is **not taken from the course material**)* | ✗ cause |\n| **Excessive surgical resection of inferior turbinates** | item 1, and the deck illustrates it with a CT captioned *"**Excessive resection of both inferior turbinates** resulting in very wide cavity. Only a small portion of the inferior turbinates is left"* | ✓ cause |\n| **Deviated nasal septum** | ⚠️ **not in `L29`’s list — and BOTH other printings of this question key it as a genuine cause**, so the other bank is preferred to outside knowledge: `entqb-nose3-108` and `entqb-nose3-109` each ask *"Secondary atrophic rhinitis is caused by all of the following except"* and each offers *"Septal deviation"* as a **true** cause. The mechanism is the wide, concave side: turbulent high-flow air dries the mucosa and it atrophies, giving a **unilateral** atrophic rhinitis *(mechanism not taken from the course material)* | ✓ cause |\n| **Scleroma** | item 3 — *"Healed stage of granulomatous disease. T.B., Scleroma."* The endpoint bank shows the endpoint: `entep-nose-98` keys the atrophic stage of rhinoscleroma as *"The nose is roomy with considerable crusts"*, clinically indistinguishable from primary atrophic rhinitis | ✓ cause |\n\n**⚠️ Not a duplicate, and recorded as such.** Q378 in this book asked the same *causes of 2ry atrophic rhinitis except* question and was folded out as a duplicate of `entqb-nose3-108`. **Q383 is a different item and is held**: its menu keeps *septal deviation* and *scleroma* but replaces *allergic rhinitis* and *radiation* with **chemotherapy** and **turbinate resection**, moving the key from *allergic rhinitis* to *chemotherapy* — a different discrimination on a different menu.\n\n**Bottom line: primary atrophic rhinitis is idiopathic — the deck lists chronic bacterial infection (*"Klebsiela, Pertussis, Diphtheria"*), vitamin A and iron deficiency, oestrogen deficiency and autoimmunity — while secondary disease always has an identifiable insult: knife, irritant, healed granuloma or beam.** Features at `enthd-nose-377`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Secondary atrophic rhinitis follows excessive surgical destruction of nasal mucosa, chronic irritants or cocaine, healed granulomatous disease (TB, scleroma) and radiotherapy — not chemotherapy.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.106. The primary and secondary aetiology lists and the turbinate-resection CT caption are transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. **⚠️ `L29` does NOT list septal deviation — it is taken as a true cause from Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose3-108` and `entqb-nose3-109`, both of which print it as a non-excepted option, in preference to outside knowledge; the drying mechanism and the chemotherapy-versus-radiotherapy contrast are tagged in the explanation. ⚠️ HELD, NOT FOLDED: Q378 asked the same question and was folded as a duplicate of `entqb-nose3-108`; Q383 prints a DIFFERENT menu with a DIFFERENT key and is retained — recorded in `house-sweep-nose.md`.** Atrophic stage of scleroma: `entep-nose-98`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-384', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-septum',
  stem:'A **33-year-old male patient** complains of **smooth bilateral nasal swelling**, severe bilateral nasal obstruction with **marked pain and tenderness over nasal bridge**. Examination revealed **rapid pulse together with hyperpyrexia**. Which of the following **characterizes this mass**?',
  options:['If neglected, it will cause septal perforation and saddle nose',"It usually shows cross-fluctuation",'It’s usually accompanied by extreme tenderness','All of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All of the above — the vignette is a SEPTAL ABSCESS, and each option is one of its three defining features.**\n\n**Reading the vignette.** A **smooth bilateral** septal swelling means a collection on **both** sides of the cartilage, because the septum is a single sheet with mucoperichondrium on each face. **Tachycardia and hyperpyrexia** are what promote it from haematoma to abscess: the endpoint bank keys exactly this discrimination — `entep-nose-8` gives a child who fell on his nose and returned **four days later with obstruction and fever**, keyed *"Septal abscess"*, while `entqb-nose7-210` gives nasal trauma with **no fever** and keys *"Septal hematoma"*. This bank asks the haematoma end of the pair at `enthd-nose-354`. `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` prescribes the search but not the signs: after nasal trauma, *"Look for **septal hematoma, abscess**"*, and *"Palpate the nose to look for : deformity, deviation, crepitus, mobility, any **tenderness**"*.\n\n| Option | Why it is true |\n|---|---|\n| **Neglected → septal perforation and saddle nose** | pus strips the perichondrium from both faces at once; the quadrilateral cartilage has **no blood supply of its own** and necroses within days. `L24` calls that cartilage the part that *"provides **support to the tip and dorsum**"* — lose it and the dorsum drops. Grade Gain keys *"Septal perforation"* and *"Nasal deformity"* as complications of septal abscess (`entqb-nose8-251`, which excludes only epistaxis) and keys saddle nose to a *"Neglected nasal hematoma (trauma)"* (`entqb-nose6-191`) |\n| **Cross-fluctuation** | the sign of a collection **continuous through or around the cartilage**: pressing one side transmits a fluid thrill to a finger on the other. ⚠️ **The term is printed by no cached deck and by no other bank in this corpus** (grep returns zero) — supplied *(not taken from the course material)* |\n| **Extreme tenderness** | the feature that separates abscess from haematoma at the bedside. A haematoma is a painless or dully aching swelling; pus under tension in a tight perichondrial pocket is exquisitely tender, and the stem’s *"marked pain and tenderness over nasal bridge"* is the same finding stated twice |\n\n**Bottom line: a bilateral septal swelling with fever is pus until proved otherwise, and it is a same-day drainage.** Treatment is **incision and drainage with systemic antibiotics**, not observation — `entep-nose-101` keys that even the sterile **haematoma** *"Needs surgical drainage"* and that treating it with *"decongestants and anti-histamines"* is wrong. The two things being raced against are the cartilage, which dies in days, and `entqb-nose8-251`’s other listed complication, **meningitis**, by veins draining backwards to the cavernous sinus.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A septal abscess presents as a smooth, exquisitely tender bilateral septal swelling with fever and tachycardia, shows cross-fluctuation, and if neglected destroys the quadrilateral cartilage to give septal perforation and a saddle nose.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.106. *"Look for septal hematoma, abscess"*, the palpation list and the quadrilateral cartilage’s support of tip and dorsum are transcribed from `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`. **⚠️ `L24` NAMES septal haematoma and abscess but prints NO clinical features for either — the fever discriminator is taken from `entep-nose-8` and `entqb-nose7-210`, the complications from `entqb-nose8-251` and `entqb-nose6-191`, and the need for drainage from `entep-nose-101`, all in preference to outside knowledge. ⚠️ *Cross-fluctuation* appears in NO deck and NO other corpus entry (grep returns zero) and is supplied from general medical knowledge, tagged in the explanation.** Haematoma sibling in this bank: `enthd-nose-354`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-385', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-septum',
  stem:'A **22-year-old female patient** presents to the ENT clinic with **unilateral nasal obstruction, sinusitis and headache**. Upon examination, it’s evident that there’s **marked adhesion between nasal septum and lateral nasal wall**. What’s your diagnosis?',
  options:['Deviated nasal septum','Nasal septal hematoma','Nasal septal perforation','Nasal synechia'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Nasal synechia — the examination finding IS the definition: a fibrous band bridging the septum to the lateral wall.**\n\n**⚠️ Synechiae are defined on no cached ENT slide** — a grep of every lecture for *synechia* and *adhesion* returns only `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`’s *"Abnormal mesodermal **adhesions** forming in the choanal area"*, which is a theory of choanal atresia, not this. **The definition is therefore taken from the other bank rather than from outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose8-254` asks what nasal synechiae are and keys *"**Adhesions between the nasal septum and turbinates**"* — the stem’s finding, word for word.\n\n**Why the three companion symptoms all follow from one band.** Two opposing **raw** surfaces healing in contact — after septal or turbinate surgery, after endoscopic sinus surgery, after packing left tight or long, after cautery of facing surfaces, or after trauma — granulate across the gap and mature into fibrous tissue *(the mechanism is `entqb-nose8-254`’s, which flags itself as unsourced by any slide)*. The band then does three things at once: it **obstructs mechanically**, and because it sits in a narrow cavity it obstructs **one side only**, which is why `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` files unilateral obstruction under *"structural abnormality or unilateral pathology"* and calls structural obstruction *"Constant"* rather than fluctuating. It **blocks the drainage route behind it** — `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` lists *"Anatomical variations"* among the predisposing factors of rhinosinusitis — which produces the **sinusitis**. And the resulting contact and sinus disease produce the **headache**: `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`’s *"contact headache"*, where *"pressure on the trigeminal nerve endings at the contact point… will trigger facial pain or headache"*.\n\n| Option | What it would have shown instead | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Deviated nasal septum** | a **displaced septal wall**, not a band between two walls — `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`: C- or S-shaped deviations, spurs, anterior dislocation, in *"80% of population"*. It obstructs by bulk, and can obstruct the **opposite** side through compensatory turbinate hypertrophy | ✗ |\n| **Nasal septal hematoma** | a **smooth bilateral swelling of the septum itself** after trauma, no adhesion (`enthd-nose-354`, `enthd-nose-384`) | ✗ |\n| **Nasal septal perforation** | a **hole**, the opposite lesion: `entqb-nose8-258` keys *"recurrent epistaxis, crustations, whistling"* to it, and it more often makes the nose feel too open than blocked | ✗ |\n| **Nasal synechia** | ✅ **keyed** | |\n\n**Bottom line: a bridge between the septum and the lateral wall is a synechia by definition, and its clinical signature is fixed unilateral obstruction with sinusitis behind it.** Management at `enthd-nose-386`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A fibrous adhesion between the nasal septum and the lateral nasal wall is a nasal synechia; it causes fixed unilateral obstruction and blocks sinus drainage behind it.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.106. **⚠️ Synechiae are defined by NO cached ENT deck (grep of all lectures returns only the choanal-atresia *"abnormal mesodermal adhesions"* of `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`) — the definition and the formation mechanism are taken from Grade Gain’s keyed `entqb-nose8-254` in preference to outside knowledge.** Unilateral/structural/constant obstruction from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; *"Anatomical variations"* as a rhinosinusitis predisposing factor from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the contact-point headache mechanism from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`; DNS features from `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`; perforation signature `entqb-nose8-258`. **⚠️ Q386 back-references this case — the two entries must stay consistent.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-386', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-septum',
  stem:'*[The bank prints this as “the previous case”; it is restated so the question stands alone:]* A **22-year-old female patient** presents with **unilateral nasal obstruction, sinusitis and headache**, and examination shows **marked adhesion between the nasal septum and the lateral nasal wall** — a **nasal synechia**. **In this case, what is the management required?**',
  options:['Submucous resection','Incision and drainage','Conservative management','Excision and release of the adhesions together with silastic sheet placement'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Divide the band, then keep the two raw surfaces apart with a silastic sheet until each has re-epithelialised — because if they touch again while raw they will simply re-fuse.**\n\n**⚠️ No cached ENT deck prints a treatment for synechiae** (grep of every lecture for *synechia* returns zero), **so the management is taken from the other bank rather than from outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose8-254` states that synechiae *"will not respond to steroids or decongestants; they are **divided surgically**, and a spacer or **silastic splint** is placed to keep the surfaces apart while they re-epithelialise"*. The keyed option is that sentence in the bank’s own words, and the **two halves are not optional extras of each other**: division alone recreates exactly the condition that formed the band in the first place — two opposing raw surfaces in a narrow cavity.\n\n| Option | What it actually treats | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Submucous resection** | the operation for a **deviated septum** — `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` lists *"Submucus resection ( SMR )"* and *"Septoplasty"* as the treatment of DNS. It removes septal cartilage and bone; here the septum is not the problem, and SMR is itself a **cause** of synechiae and of septal perforation (`entqb-nose8-249` keys *"Iatrogenic after SMR operation"* as the commonest cause of perforation) | ✗ — would make things worse |\n| **Incision and drainage** | the operation for a **collection** — septal abscess or haematoma (`enthd-nose-384`, `entep-nose-101`). There is nothing to drain in a solid fibrous band | ✗ |\n| **Conservative management** | fails by definition: the band is **mature fibrous tissue**, so steroids, decongestants and douches cannot dissolve it. Note the contrast with the mucosal rhinitides in this same chapter, where conservative treatment is the whole answer — rhinitis medicamentosa reverses on *"Saline washes and topical steroids"* (`L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`, `enthd-nose-379`) | ✗ |\n| **Excision and release of the adhesions together with silastic sheet placement** | ✅ **keyed** | |\n\n**Bottom line: cut it, then splint it — and prevention beats both.** `entqb-nose8-254`’s closing rule is *"avoid opposing raw areas, and use splints where the cavity is tight"*, which matters most after endoscopic sinus surgery, where a synechia across the middle meatus can reproduce the very obstruction and recurrent sinusitis the operation was performed to relieve — the ostiomeatal complex being the shared drainage channel of the whole anterior sinus group (`enthd-nose-367` chapter context; complex components at `enthd-nose-322`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Nasal synechiae are treated by surgical division of the adhesion with placement of a silastic sheet or splint to keep the raw surfaces apart until they re-epithelialise; medical treatment cannot dissolve a fibrous band.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.107. **⚠️ Back-references “the previous case” = `enthd-nose-385` (p.106, nasal synechia); the case is restated in the stem so the entry stands alone in a shuffled deck. ⚠️ NO cached ENT deck prints any treatment for synechiae (grep of all lectures returns zero) — division plus silastic splint, the failure of medical treatment, and the prevention rule are all taken from Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose8-254` in preference to outside knowledge.** SMR and septoplasty as the DNS operations from `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`; SMR as the commonest cause of septal perforation `entqb-nose8-249`; saline/steroid reversal of rhinitis medicamentosa from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-387', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-septum',
  stem:'An infant was born with **congenital cleft palate as his LEFT palate was deficient**, which led to **2ry deviated nasal septum**. Which of the following best describes the **nasal septal deviation** in this case?',
  options:['The septum will deviate towards right side','The septum will deviate towards left side','The septum will deviate to both sides','The septum will deviate to any side'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The bank keys the DEFICIENT side — the septum falls towards the left, the side that has lost its floor.**\n\n**The deck’s framework, which is as far as the course goes.** `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` gives developmental deviation three headings — *"a-Birth mouldin theory · b-**Abnormal intrauterine postures with compression forces acting on the nose and upper jaws** · c-Post natal trauma"* — and adds *"4- **Mass effect**"* as a separate aetiology. It also gives the anatomy that the argument turns on: the septum’s *"Inferior border of vomer **articulates with nasal crest formed by maxillae and palatine bones**"*, and the septal cartilage *"provides support to the tip and dorsum"*. **In a cleft palate the maxillary and palatine shelves have not fused, so on the cleft side that nasal crest — the septum’s floor and anchor — is simply not there.** Unopposed growth and compression from the intact right side then push the septum into the side that offers no resistance, and the septum grows deviated rather than being bent later. That is the deck’s *compression forces* and *mass effect* logic applied to an asymmetrical palate *(the application itself is **not taken from the course material** — no cached ENT deck links cleft palate to septal deviation, and a grep of the corpus finds no other bank printing it either)*.\n\n**⚠️ RECORD, KEY KEPT: the commonly reported cleft finding runs the other way for part of the septum.** In unilateral cleft lip and palate the **caudal (anterior) septum** is usually described as deviating **away from the cleft, into the non-cleft nostril**, while the posterior septum bows towards the cleft — so an answer of *right* is defensible for the front of the septum *(also not taken from the course material)*. The bank prints a single-side answer, and the `answer` is left exactly as printed; the discrepancy is recorded here rather than disputed.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Towards right side** | ✗ as keyed — but see the caudal-septum note above |\n| **Towards left side** | ✅ **keyed** — towards the deficient/cleft side |\n| **To both sides** | ✗ a septum can be **S-shaped** and so convex to both sides at different levels — `L24` prints *"Cartilagenous, C or S shaped deviations either in vertical or horizontal plane"* — but that is a shape, not the direction produced by a one-sided defect |\n| **To any side** | ✗ the whole point of the stem is that the deviation is **determined**, not random: name the deficient side and you have named the direction |\n\n**Bottom line: a one-sided structural deficiency gives a predictable, one-sided deviation, and it is developmental rather than traumatic.** Clinically the consequences are `L24`’s: obstruction *"on the same side of the deviation or opposite side because of the hypertrophic changes in turbinate"*, snoring, *"dryness, crusting and epistaxis"*, headache and sinusitis — and the palate itself adds **rhinolalia aperta** (`entep-nose-109` keys cleft palate as a cause).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A unilateral cleft palate removes the septum’s bony floor on the cleft side, so the secondary septal deviation is towards the deficient side — the direction is determined by the defect, not random.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.107. The developmental aetiologies, *"Mass effect"*, the vomer–nasal crest articulation, the C/S-shaped deviations and the symptom list are transcribed from `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`; rhinolalia aperta from cleft palate is `entep-nose-109`. **⚠️ GAP FILLED AND TAGGED: no cached ENT deck links cleft palate to septal deviation and no other bank in the corpus prints it — the mechanism is supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged. ⚠️ ARGUABLE KEY RECORDED, KEY KEPT: the caudal septum in unilateral cleft lip and palate is usually described as deviating AWAY from the cleft, which would make option a defensible; both readings are set out in the explanation and the `answer` is left as the bank prints it.** The stem is printed in the book with a stray leading full stop (*". An infant was born…"*), dropped here as a numbering artefact.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-388', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-septum',
  stem:'A **single fracture** affecting nasal septum **but NOT involving the osseocartilaginous junction** is considered which **type of septal fractures**?',
  options:['Type 1','Type 2','Type 3','Type 4'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Type 1 — one fracture line, and it stays on one side of the bony-cartilaginous junction. That makes it the STABLE type.**\n\n**⚠️ No cached ENT deck prints this classification.** `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` reaches the word and stops: under *"Classification"* it lists only *"Nature of injury · Extent of deformity · **Pattern of fracture**"* — three headings with nothing beneath them, and no numbered types anywhere in the deck. **The grading is therefore taken from the other bank rather than from outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose8-236` asks what describes a **Type 1** nasal fracture and keys *"**A single fracture line not crossing the osteocartilaginous junction, considered stable**"* — the stem of this question, restated.\n\n**Shared table for this question and its pair `enthd-nose-389`:**\n\n| Type | Definition | Stability | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Type 1** | ✅ **keyed here** — a **single** fracture line, **not** crossing the osseocartilaginous junction | **stable** | `entqb-nose8-236` |\n| **Type 2** | a **single** fracture line that **does** cross the junction | **less stable** | `entqb-nose8-239`, keyed at `enthd-nose-389` |\n| **Type 3** | **multiple** fractures, which may cross the junction | **least stable — highest rate of complications** | `entqb-nose8-240`, `entqb-nose8-237` |\n| **Type 4** | ⚠️ **printed as an option by this bank and defined by NOTHING** — neither `L24` nor any Grade Gain item prints a Type 4, and `entqb-nose8-237` asks which type has the highest complication rate on a menu of Types 1–3 only. It functions here as an empty distractor | — | not printed anywhere |\n\n**Why the junction is the dividing line.** `L24` gives the anatomy: the septum is *"Membranous"*, *"Cartilagenous portion composed of quadrilateral cartilage"* which *"provides support to the tip and dorsum"*, and *"Bony septum : mainly by perpendicular plate of ethmoid and vomer"*, whose *"Anterior border articulates with perpendicular plate of ethmoid **and septal cartilage inferiorly**"*. That articulation is the **keystone** where the flexible cartilage is fixed to rigid bone. A fracture that stays within one material leaves the keystone intact and the septum still supported; a fracture that crosses it unlocks the cartilage from the bone, which is why the grades run in that order.\n\n**Clinical consequence, in the deck’s words.** `L24`: *"Most patients don’t need any active treatment in mild condition just observation"*, with closed reduction under GA or LA where needed and open reduction (*"Septorhinoplasty"*) reserved for *"fractures with dislocation of nasal dorsum and **significant septal deformity**"*. A Type 1 is the mild end of that spectrum.\n\n**⚠️ This is one half of a deliberate contrast pair with `enthd-nose-389`; the entire difference between the two keys is the word *not*.**\n\n**Note as printed:** this bank spells it *"osseocartilaginous"* and Grade Gain spells it *"osteocartilaginous"* — the same junction.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A single septal fracture line that does not cross the osseocartilaginous junction is Type 1 — the stable grade — because the keystone fixing cartilage to bone is left intact.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.107. **⚠️ `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` prints the heading *"Classification: Nature of injury · Extent of deformity · Pattern of fracture"* with NO numbered types beneath it — the Type 1/2/3 definitions are taken from Grade Gain’s keyed `entqb-nose8-236`, `entqb-nose8-239`, `entqb-nose8-240` and `entqb-nose8-237` in preference to outside knowledge. ⚠️ NO source in this project defines a Type 4; it is recorded as an undefined distractor rather than invented.** Septal anatomy, the vomer–cartilage articulation and the treatment ladder are transcribed from `L24`. **⚠️ Deliberate contrast pair with `enthd-nose-389` (Type 2, crossing the junction) — the two must NOT be merged; the shared type table lives in this entry.** Printed spelling *"osseocartilaginous"* kept; Grade Gain prints *"osteocartilaginous"*.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-389', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-septum',
  stem:'A **single fracture** affecting nasal septum **AND involving the osseocartilaginous junction** is considered which **type of septal fractures**?',
  options:['Type 1','Type 2','Type 3','Type 4'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Type 2 — still a single fracture line, but it crosses the osseocartilaginous junction, and crossing it is what costs the septum its stability.**\n\n`entqb-nose8-239` asks what is true of a **Type 2** nasal fracture and keys *"**The fracture line crosses the osteocartilaginous junction, making it less stable**"*, explicitly rejecting *"It is considered the most stable type"* and *"It has the highest rate of complications"* — Type 2 is neither the best nor the worst grade. **⚠️ The classification is printed by no cached ENT deck; the full four-row table and its sourcing live in `enthd-nose-388`, and are not repeated here.**\n\n**⚠️ This entry and `enthd-nose-388` are a deliberate contrast pair printed back to back on the same page with an identical option menu. The stems differ by one word — *not involving* versus *involving* — and that word is the whole teaching point.** Read together they say: **the number of fracture lines and their relationship to the keystone are two separate variables**, and the classification grades the second one first.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Type 1** | ✗ the sibling question — a single line that **spares** the junction, and the **stable** grade (`enthd-nose-388`, `entqb-nose8-236`) |\n| **Type 2** | ✅ **keyed** |\n| **Type 3** | ✗ requires **multiple** fractures; `entqb-nose8-240` keys it *"The **least stable** type with multiple fractures, Can be crossing the osteocartilaginous junction"*, and `entqb-nose8-237` keys **Type 3** as the type with the **highest rate of complications**. The stem says *single*, so Type 3 is excluded on the count alone |\n| **Type 4** | ✗ defined by **no source in this project** — not by `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`, which prints no numbered types at all, and not by any Grade Gain item; `entqb-nose8-237` runs its menu to Type 3 only |\n\n**Why *less stable* is the clinically operative phrase.** `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` records that after trauma *"The septum and nasal bones may **buckle on itself, fracture vertically, horizontally or get crushed**"*, and that *"Fracture of septal cartilage or its **dislocation** can occur without nasal bones fracture in cases of trauma to lower nose"*. Once the fracture separates cartilage from bone at the keystone, the quadrilateral cartilage — the part that *"provides support to the tip and dorsum"* — is free to displace, so the deformity tends to progress and to recur after closed reduction. That is why the deck reserves open reduction, *"Open reduction ( Septorhinoplasty)"*, for *"fractures with dislocation of nasal dorsum and significant septal deformity"*, and why splints are mentioned: *"Splints may be necessary"*.\n\n**Bottom line: count the lines, then ask whether any of them crosses the keystone. One line sparing it = Type 1; one line crossing it = Type 2; several lines = Type 3.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A single septal fracture line that crosses the osseocartilaginous junction is Type 2 — less stable than Type 1 because the cartilage is unlocked from the bony septum, but not the multiple-fracture Type 3.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.107. **⚠️ The classification appears in NO cached ENT deck — `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` prints only the headings *"Nature of injury · Extent of deformity · Pattern of fracture"*. Type 2 is taken from Grade Gain’s keyed `entqb-nose8-239`, Type 3 from `entqb-nose8-240` and `entqb-nose8-237`, Type 1 from `entqb-nose8-236`, in preference to outside knowledge; no source defines a Type 4. Shared type table is in `enthd-nose-388` and deliberately not repeated here.** The buckling/dislocation description, the tip-and-dorsum support, splints and the open-reduction indication are transcribed from `L24`. **⚠️ Deliberate contrast pair with `enthd-nose-388` — do NOT merge; the discriminating word is *involving*.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-390', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **only type of sensory receptors found in nasal mucosa**?',
  options:['Touch receptors','Proprioceptors','Pain receptors','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Pain receptors — the nasal mucosa carries free nerve endings and essentially nothing else, which is why every nasal sensation that is not smell reaches you as irritation or pain.**\n\n**The wiring, from the decks.** `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` gives the nose only two sensory jobs — *"**Olfactory function**: in the olfactory area"* and, among *"Other functions"*, *"**Reflex function**"* — and it makes the olfactory area a separate territory, *"the upper part of the nose related to the superior turbinate, cribriform plate… lined by columnar non ciliated cells"* (`enthd-nose-238`). Everything below that is respiratory mucosa, and its general sensory supply is the **trigeminal**: Grade Gain keys the nerve supply of the nose as *"Olfactory nerve and trigeminal nerve (ophthalmic V1 and maxillary V2)"* (`entqb-nose1-26`). `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` shows what that supply actually delivers — headaches *"triggered by **nasal irritation** (stimulation of the trigeminal nerve)"*, and a *"contact headache"* in which *"pressure on the **trigeminal nerve endings** at the contact point by a deviated septum or spur will trigger facial pain"*. **Nerve endings**, not corpuscles.\n\n**⚠️ The receptor question itself is a gap and is filled here** *(not taken from the course material — no cached deck classifies nasal receptors, and a grep of the whole corpus for *pain receptor* returns zero)*: respiratory nasal mucosa contains **free nerve endings (nociceptors and chemo-irritant endings)** and no encapsulated mechanoreceptors, so the modality it can report is nociception.\n\n| Option | Why not | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Touch receptors** | Meissner and Merkel corpuscles are **skin** receptors. The only part of the nose that is skin is the **vestibule** — `L23`’s *"most anterior part of the nose"* — and the stem asks about **mucosa**. It is also why patients localise an intranasal foreign body so badly while feeling a vestibular one precisely | ✗ |\n| **Proprioceptors** | muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs and joint receptors — a mucous membrane has **no muscles and no joints** to report on | ✗ |\n| **Pain receptors** | ✅ **keyed** | |\n| **None of the above** | ✗ excluded by the key, and by the everyday observation that the nose hurts |\n\n**Bottom line: smell is a special sense with its own epithelium; everything else the nose feels is trigeminal nociception.** That single fact explains why nasal cautery, packing and endoscopy demand topical anaesthesia; why irritants provoke the **sneeze** reflex, whose afferent limb Grade Gain keys as trigeminal V1 (`entqb-nose1-29`) and which `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` lists among the nose’s *"Defence- Mechanisms… Reflexes (cough, sneeze)"*; and why a septal spur touching a turbinate produces *pain* rather than a sensation of touch (`L32`’s contact headache; `enthd-nose-385`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Nasal mucosa carries only free nerve endings — pain/irritant receptors supplied by the trigeminal nerve — with no touch corpuscles or proprioceptors; smell is served separately by the olfactory epithelium.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.107. The nose’s functions, the olfactory-area boundary and the vestibule as the anterior skin-lined part are from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; trigeminal irritation and the contact-point headache from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`; the sneeze reflex among the defence mechanisms from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; nerve supply `entqb-nose1-26` and sneeze afferent `entqb-nose1-29`. **⚠️ GAP FILLED AND TAGGED: no cached ENT deck classifies the sensory receptors of nasal mucosa and no other bank in the corpus prints them (*pain receptor* returns zero over `questions.ent.js`) — the free-nerve-ending answer and the reasons the other two receptor classes are absent are supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.**' }
